Acer Nitro V16 WMI Drivers (nitro-wmi)
======================================

This repository contains out-of-tree Linux kernel modules for the
Acer Nitro:

    acer-wmi-battery    Battery health mode (the charge limiter) and
                        battery temperature.
    acer-wmi-fan        CPU and GPU fan speed control and fan RPM
                        monitoring.

Both modules are built and loaded separately, and neither depends on the
in-tree acer-wmi driver.


Special Thanks
--------------

A very big thank you to Frederik Harwath (frederik-h).

acer-wmi-battery in this repository is built directly on his original
acer-wmi-battery driver:

    https://github.com/frederik-h/acer-wmi-battery/

This project would not have a battery module without his work. His code
and its clean structure made it possible to adapt the driver to the Nitro
V16, and everything that works in the battery module stands on the
foundation he built. Thank you, Frederik!

Thanks also to Jelle van der Waa, co-author of the original driver.

The changes made here are small: a DMI entry for the Acer Nitro ANV16-71,
and devm_kzalloc() instead of devm_kzalloc_obj() so the module builds
against kernel headers that do not provide the newer helper. The original
copyright and license are kept.


Supported Hardware
------------------

    Acer Nitro V16, DMI product name "Nitro ANV16-71"

Tested on Fedora with kernel 7.3.3.

    * acer-wmi-fan only loads on the supported model unless the force
      parameter is used (see "Module Parameters").
    * acer-wmi-battery exposes the charge limiter on the supported model
      only. Other systems get battery temperature reporting only, unless
      the force parameter is used.

acer-wmi-battery uses the newer WMI buffer API of the kernel
(struct wmi_buffer and wmidev_invoke_method()), so older kernels may not
be able to build it.


System Prerequisites
--------------------

    * Kernel headers that match the running kernel
      (kernel-devel on Fedora, linux-headers on Debian and Ubuntu).
    * gcc and make.

Check that the running kernel and the installed headers are the same
version: "uname -r" must match the version of the headers.


Building and Loading
--------------------

Build as a normal user. Do not use sudo for make, because that leaves
root-owned build files behind.

    make

Load the modules:

    sudo insmod acer-wmi-battery.ko
    sudo insmod acer-wmi-fan.ko

Unload the modules:

    sudo rmmod acer_wmi_fan
    sudo rmmod acer_wmi_battery

Unloading acer-wmi-fan returns the fans to automatic mode.

Secure Boot: unsigned modules are rejected while Secure Boot is enabled
("Key was rejected by service"). Disable Secure Boot or sign the modules
with your own key.

Modules loaded with insmod are gone after a reboot. To keep them, install
them for the running kernel and load them at boot:

    sudo mkdir -p /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/extra
    sudo cp src/*.ko /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/extra/
    sudo depmod -a
    printf 'acer_wmi_battery\nacer_wmi_fan\n' | sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/nitro-wmi.conf

This must be repeated after every kernel update, because a module is tied
to the kernel version it was built for.


How to use?
-----------

1. Battery Health (acer-wmi-battery)

Acer's battery health mode keeps the battery from charging to full, which
preserves battery health on laptops that are mostly used plugged into AC
power. The limit itself is decided by the firmware and cannot be set to
a custom value with this driver.

The mode is exposed as the charge type of the battery:

    Standard    Normal charging.
    Long_Life   Battery health mode enabled.

To check the current charge type:

    cat /sys/class/power_supply/BAT*/charge_types

To enable battery health mode:

    echo Long_Life | sudo tee /sys/class/power_supply/BAT*/charge_types

To disable it again:

    echo Standard | sudo tee /sys/class/power_supply/BAT*/charge_types

The battery is BAT1 on the tested machine. The battery temperature is also
reported by the module:

    cat /sys/class/power_supply/BAT*/temp
    sensors

Whether the setting is kept by the firmware after a reboot or a shutdown
depends on the laptop. Check "charge_types" after a reboot. Set Standard
before removing the module, because the firmware may keep the state while
the driver is not loaded to change it.

2. Fan Speed (acer-wmi-fan)

Controls the CPU and GPU fan speeds as "cpu,gpu" percentages:

    0       Auto (the firmware fan curve).
    20-99   Fixed speed.
    100     Maximum speed. If both fans are set to 100, the firmware
            maximum mode is used.

Speeds of 1-19 are refused unless the module was loaded with
unsafe_low_duty=1.

Example (set CPU to 50 and GPU to 70):

    echo 50,70 | sudo tee /sys/module/acer_wmi_fan/parameters/fan_speed

Set CPU to automatic and GPU to a fixed 60:

    echo 0,60 | sudo tee /sys/module/acer_wmi_fan/parameters/fan_speed

Return both fans to automatic:

    echo 0,0 | sudo tee /sys/module/acer_wmi_fan/parameters/fan_speed

To check the requested speeds (these are the values the driver last
applied, not a measurement):

    sudo cat /sys/module/acer_wmi_fan/parameters/fan_speed

To read the real fan speed in RPM, printed as "cpu,gpu":

    sudo cat /sys/module/acer_wmi_fan/parameters/fan_rpm

